          { name: "Housing Affordability and Renter Constituency Engagement", tag: "lean-into", why: "VA HD-5's 56.5% renter rate in a $131,347-income district is a critical strategic signal—these are educated, affluent professionals who are not buying, which indicates either that local housing costs exceed their purchase capacity or that they have deliberately chosen rental mobility. Housing affordability, tenant protections, and rental market regulation are not secondary policy issues but the direct material reality of more than half the district. An incumbent who can point to specific legislative accomplishments on housing policy, renter protection, and affordability initiatives speaks directly to this constituency's lived experience." },
          { name: "Policy Implementation and Constituent Service Responsiveness", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 72.6% college education and $131,347 median income, VA HD-5 is a constituency that evaluates incumbents on policy substance and government responsiveness, not on partisan loyalty or inherited voting patterns. This constituency notices when state agencies fail to respond to constituent problems, when promised initiatives stall, and when representatives are not actively working to implement their stated agenda. Visible constituent service capacity, tracking of agency responsiveness, and demonstrated legislative follow-through on district priorities are the mechanisms that sustain a 95% margin in an engaged, educated district." },
          { name: "Demographic Diversity Without Numerical Dominance", tag: "careful", why: "VA HD-5 is genuinely diverse—56.38% white, 17.56% Black, 16.07% Hispanic, 4.92% Asian—without any single group commanding clear numerical majority, and no single demographic constituency can be taken for granted. In a 95% Democratic district, the margin is built on sustained engagement across all major communities; perceived neglect of any single demographic group—whether white, Black, or Hispanic—creates opening for primary challenge, activist pressure, or constituent mobilization. This requires visible, ongoing legislative attention and constituent service across all communities, not generic minority outreach or assumption of white voter loyalty." },
          { name: "Treating 95% as a Durable Safe Harbor", tag: "avoid", why: "A 95% margin in a district this educated, affluent, and engaged is not a passive floor but an active mandate that assumes ongoing responsiveness and policy delivery. An incumbent who campaigns or governs as though the margin is secure regardless of performance is misreading the constituency's actual posture toward accountability. In a district where 72.6% of adults are college-educated and the median household income is $131,347, constituents have the information access and civic sophistication to organize alternative candidates if they conclude an incumbent is not delivering." },
          { name: "Partisan Messaging Over Constituent Problem-Solving", tag: "avoid", why: "VA HD-5 is a constituency that votes Democratic consistently (95%) but does not vote primarily on partisan messaging—it votes on whether government is solving concrete problems: housing affordability, transit access, school quality, agency responsiveness, and implementation of stated priorities. An incumbent who relies on partisan framing or generic Democratic messaging rather than demonstrating specific constituent service and policy results misses the actual evaluation criteria this district uses. In a 95% Democratic district, primary challenges are more likely to come from the left (on policy substance and implementation speed) than from Republican opposition, and they will focus on whether the incumbent is delivering, not on partisan alignment." }
