          { name: "Treating Affluence as Ideological Consensus", tag: "avoid", why: "A 67% Democratic result in an affluent district reflects educated voters' support for public investment, infrastructure, and progressive taxation — not permission to govern further left than the district's actual preferences or to treat the electorate's prosperity as evidence of unfairness. Messaging that implies the district's high income is the problem, or that frames affluence as a liability to be addressed through punitive policy, will alienate rather than energize a constituency that supports progressive outcomes but is not self-flagellating about its own success. This district rewards competence and practical problem-solving, not ideological purity." },
          { name: "Assuming 67% Is a Durable Lock Permitting Governance Drift", tag: "avoid", why: "The 67% margin is healthy but not a supermajority in a district where one-third of voters actively chose the opposition and where the electorate's high education and income mean they evaluate on demonstrated competence rather than inherited partisan loyalty. A representative who consolidates only the existing Democratic base and drifts into governance patterns that diverge from the district's actual fiscal and professional-economy priorities is vulnerable to a credible Republican challenger who offers fiscal discipline and institutional competence to an affluent, educated electorate capable of splitting its vote when dissatisfied." }
