          { name: "Renter and Immigrant-Origin Professional Integration", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 32.2% renter rate in a median-income-$158,753 district signals young professional renters and possibly immigrant-origin residents whose economic positioning differs materially from established homeowners. At 13.51% Hispanic and 11.61% Asian in a high-income area, these populations likely reflect professional immigration (doctors, engineers, entrepreneurs) rather than working-class settlement; their concerns around housing affordability, credential recognition, and economic integration pathways are real and present. Serving this population with specific policy attention — housing development that serves young professionals, streamlined licensing for immigrant professionals, economic development that connects immigrant entrepreneurs to capital — consolidates a constituency that could otherwise feel excluded from a homeowner-centered political economy." },
          { name: "Fiscal Discipline and Cost-Benefit Governance", tag: "careful", why: "The 25% Republican opposition in a 75-25 district is likely concentrated among older homeowners and business-oriented residents who prioritize tax efficiency, measured spending, and results-driven governance. While electorally marginal, this constituency serves as an early warning signal: if the incumbent's record appears to involve wasteful spending, ideologically driven budgeting, or failure to demonstrate cost-benefit discipline, defection among these voters can signal broader coalition weakness. Maintaining credibility with fiscal conservatives within a Democratic coalition requires demonstrating that progressive priorities are pursued with discipline and measurable outcomes, not reflexive spending." },
          { name: "Partisan Polarization as a Governing Framework", tag: "avoid", why: "The 75% Democratic margin and 72.8% college attainment can create a temptation to govern as though the district's Democratic identity is inherent to its values rather than a contingent alignment between Democratic policy positions and the material concerns of this particular constituency. If the incumbent frames governance through a partisan lens — treating opposition as morally deficient rather than materially different — or treats the 25% opposition as illegitimate, credibility erodes even among the 75% coalition. Affluent, educated constituencies expect governance that is analytically serious and respectful of dissent, not partisan tribalism." },
          { name: "Treating 75% as Permission to Ignore Fiscal Constraints or Policy Tradeoffs", tag: "avoid", why: "A 75% margin can create complacency about the need for rigorous policy analysis and honest communication of tradeoffs. When proposals are justified primarily by reference to Democratic values or moral imperatives, rather than to specific fiscal sustainability, measurable outcomes, and honest cost accounting, the incumbent signals to voters that their intelligence is not being respected. This is particularly damaging in a district with 72.8% college attainment, where voters are capable of identifying policy incoherence and where perceived dishonesty or hand-waving creates durable reputational damage." }
