          "Do not compete for the 73%—build credible opposition by engaging the 27%'s actual concerns and then expanding methodically: Republican voters in VA-HD-11 are predominantly college-educated professionals with high incomes; they are not responding to cultural wedge messaging or anti-government rhetoric. They are evaluating on specific fiscal policy (tax burden, business environment, transportation funding), regulatory burden on professional services, and whether their children's educational and economic opportunities are being protected.",
          "Address the Asian professional community directly with economic and immigration specificity, not cultural messaging: At 21.84% of the district, Asian voters are not a marginal constituency—they are a principal demographic group that has moved Democratic. Any Republican who wants to build credibility in VA-HD-11 must engage Asian professionals on skilled immigration policy, business environment competitiveness, and education quality with genuine fluency, not token acknowledgment.",
          "Treat the 40.8% renter rate as a political vulnerability signal, not an irrelevant statistic: In a district with $134k median income, 40.8% renters suggests younger professionals and families—and the fact that they are renting in an expensive market indicates housing cost pressure despite high income. A Republican message on housing costs, zoning reform, and affordability that speaks to this younger constituency with specificity could open a lane if Democrats appear indifferent to affordability constraints."
