          { name: "Homeowner-Dominant District & Property Tax Pressure", tag: "careful", why: "At 10.6% renter rate, VA-HD-22 is a homeowner-dominant district where property tax policy, school funding mechanisms, and local development decisions directly affect household wealth and community character. While median income is high, Northern Virginia property values and tax burdens are among the nation's steepest; homeowners in this district are acutely aware of tax-to-services ratios and whether local government delivers value. This constituency can split its vote on tax fairness and local fiscal management even when strongly Democratic on statewide or national issues, making property tax and education funding efficiency both a loyalty test and a vulnerability for any incumbent who appears inattentive to fiscal discipline." },
          { name: "Treating the District as Safely Democratic", tag: "avoid", why: "A 55% Democratic result in a high-income, highly educated, majority-homeowner suburban district is not a safe Democratic seat — it is a persuadable constituency that has not been captured by either party. Northern Virginia suburbs have shifted Democratic in recent cycles, but this shift reflects coalition-building, not ideological conversion; affluent homeowners with substantial assets remain responsive to arguments about government efficiency, tax fairness, and neighborhood stability. Any Democratic incumbent who campaigns this seat as though 55% represents a durable base rather than a coalition to be actively maintained will find the margin eroded by Republican investment in fiscal and property-tax messaging." },
          { name: "Identity-Based Messaging Over Economic Specificity", tag: "avoid", why: "While VA-HD-22's diversity and college-educated composition creates receptivity to equity-focused messaging, this constituency is ultimately motivated by whether government is delivering concrete results: school quality, safe neighborhoods, efficient services, and policies that preserve or enhance property values and opportunity. Candidates who rely on identity affirmation or abstract equity rhetoric without tying it to specific economic outcomes — housing affordability, school funding mechanisms, transportation infrastructure — will find even a diverse, highly educated constituency skeptical. This is a district that rewards specificity and competence over symbolism, and messaging that prioritizes identity affirmation over material governance outcomes misreads the constituency's actual values and decision-making criteria." }
