          { name: "Treating the 76% Result as a Permanent Floor Requiring No Active Constituency Engagement", tag: "avoid", why: "A 76% result is a strong majority, but it is not a mandate requiring no further work — it is evidence of successful coalition-building that demands maintenance and deepening. A representative who campaigns primarily to consolidate the existing 76% without actively engaging the material concerns of renters, Black constituents, immigrant communities, and young professionals, or who treats these constituencies as permanent Democratic voters who require no specific legislative attention, risks allowing a credible challenger to grow opposition by identifying and addressing neglected material concerns." },
          { name: "Generic Progressive Positioning Without District-Specific Policy Translation", tag: "avoid", why: "VA-HD-23's demographic profile — affluent, educated, young, diverse, urban — can encourage abstract progressive rhetoric disconnected from the specific material concerns that these constituencies actually face. Housing affordability for educated renters, racial equity outcomes in public education, worker protections for immigrants in professional and service sectors, and neighborhood character preservation during development are not generic progressive values; they are concrete policy domains where constituents evaluate a representative's capacity to translate alignment into governing results. A candidate who substitutes abstract positioning for concrete legislative agenda and specific policy proposals will find that the district's demographic alignment does not automatically translate into sustained electoral support." }
