          "The district's competitive vulnerability, if it exists at all, lies in the intersection of housing costs, rental market pressure, and the specific economic concerns of its plural-majority communities. At 18.3% renters and a median age of 36.3, the district includes a meaningful population of younger professionals and families for whom $125,846 household median income represents an average that masks real vulnerability among younger renters and single-income households to Arlington's rapid escalation in housing costs. The Latino community (27.37%) has distinct economic priorities around workforce participation and wage floors; the Black community (22.9%) evaluates on economic mobility and public safety; the Asian community (10.36%) prioritizes education and professional-opportunity access. A 91% Democratic margin reflects strong current alignment on these communities' perceived needs, but that alignment is contingent on representation that takes each community's specific concerns seriously rather than absorbing them into a generic minority-community strategy. The path to competitiveness in VA-HD-24 is not ideological opposition but rather a credible alternative on schools, housing, and economic opportunity that specific communities believe will serve them better."
