          { name: "Economic Competence and Constituent Service", tag: "lean-into", why: "A district with 26.1% college attainment and affluent demographics evaluates representatives on demonstrated professional competence: do constituent service requests get answered, is the legislator's office organized and responsive, are local governance problems being addressed with specificity rather than broad rhetoric? This is a constituency that respects the machinery of governance and responds to candidates who demonstrate they can operate it effectively." },
          { name: "Minority Community Engagement in an Affluent District", tag: "careful", why: "The 9.2% non-white population (5.46% Hispanic, 4.12% Black, 0.62% Asian) and the 28% Democratic opposition suggest that minority communities and renters in VA-HD-35 experience the district differently than the affluent homeowner majority—housing affordability, education equity, economic mobility are their likely focal concerns. These communities should receive active constituent service and policy attention; neglecting them risks creating a more organized opposition base for future challengers." },
          { name: "Treating 72% as License to Disregard Opposition", tag: "avoid", why: "A 72-28 result is large but not a unanimous mandate; the 28% opposition in an educated, affluent district represents 28% of informed voters who made a deliberate choice. Campaigns or governance that frame the result as permission to ignore minority interests or smaller constituencies create the political opening for future challengers to organize that opposition into a credible threat, particularly if economic conditions change or specific governance failures occur." },
          { name: "Treating the District as Having Demographic Complexity It Does Not Have", tag: "avoid", why: "Unlike districts with significant unmeasured AIAN or immigrant populations, VA-HD-35's Census categories account for ~99% of the population, and the small minority communities present are not swing constituencies capable of determining election outcomes. Overweighting minority engagement strategy, or framing the district's politics around demographic diversity rather than around affluent homeowner economic interests, misreads the actual political geography and risks appearing inauthentic to the constituency that determines elections." }
