          { name: "Renter Constituency Economic Opportunity", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 32.4% renter rate in a working-class district means nearly one-third of constituents are evaluating on housing affordability, neighborhood quality, access to transportation and services, and whether government is improving their economic mobility — not on property-owning household concerns. Renters in Virginia districts have demonstrated capacity to swing elections when offered credible alternatives focused on their material circumstances rather than inherited partisan identity. In a 58-42 district, improving renter engagement or candidate preference by four to five percentage points is sufficient to shift the seat; this population deserves active legislative attention and constituent communication rather than being treated as a secondary demographic." },
          { name: "Native American Community Governance Partnership", tag: "careful", why: "The 4.96% AIAN gap, while smaller than in some Virginia districts, represents a constituent population whose relationship to state representation is mediated by tribal sovereignty, Indian Health Service access, economic development, and healthcare policy. Native American communities in Virginia evaluate heavily on whether legislators treat tribal governance as a legitimate institutional partner rather than as a special interest competing with mainstream constituents. At 58-42, losing active engagement with this population — or allowing a challenger to organize it around specific unmet governance needs — is sufficient to shift the outcome in the next competitive cycle." },
          { name: "Treating 58% as a Safe Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "A 58-42 result is not a landslide or a safe seat in Virginia's contemporary political context — it is a solid holding that requires active constituency engagement across homeowners, renters, and Native American communities to remain durable. Any incumbent or challenger who campaigns as though 58% represents a comfortable base and neglects the opposition's driving concerns is arithmetically overconfident; the renter population and tribal constituencies are both capable of organizing alternative support if they perceive the incumbent as dismissive of their material interests." },
          { name: "Misunderstanding Renter Voter Behavior as Inherited Partisanship", tag: "avoid", why: "A common strategic error in working-class districts is assuming renters are inherently Democratic and therefore do not merit engagement from Republican incumbents — when the actual dynamic is that renters evaluate on material economic delivery regardless of party label. Campbell's 58% depends on keeping renter defection below 15-20% of that population; any communications strategy that ignores or minimizes renter concerns in favor of homeowner messaging is ceding the opportunity to build a more durable majority and unnecessarily exposing the seat to challenger organization around housing affordability and service access." }
