          { name: "Non-College-Educated Workforce & Wage Stagnation", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 17.2% college-attainment rate (among the lowest in the state) indicates a constituency whose economic position depends primarily on wages rather than credential-based income or asset appreciation. At $51,290 median household income, wage growth, job quality, and economic mobility for non-college-educated workers are the determining factors in whether this constituency experiences economic improvement or decline. A representative who engages the material conditions of working-age, non-college-educated workers—particularly younger adults in this category—with specific policy proposals (apprenticeships, manufacturing support, union organizing protection, wage standards) can build credibility and shift voting behavior in a district where educational attainment is low." },
          { name: "The 22.4% Renter Constituency", tag: "careful", why: "Renters represent nearly one-quarter of HD-47's population, and their economic concerns (housing affordability, wage stagnation, service-sector job quality) are materially different from the homeowner majority. In a district where the incumbent's coalition is built on homeowner messaging, renters can become an invisible constituency whose frustration with the representative's responsiveness to their specific concerns creates vulnerability to a challenger who engages them directly. At 22.4%, this is not a marginal group—it is a meaningful constituency that any strategic analysis must address separately from the homeowner narrative." },
          { name: "Treating 74% as a Durable Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "A 74-26 result in a district with these demographic characteristics—older, non-college-educated, moderate income—appears to be a strong Republican margin but is not durable if the incumbent becomes disconnected from the specific economic concerns of the aging homeowner majority or if a challenger mobilizes younger, working-age, or renter voters with authentic economic messaging. The 26% Democratic share is substantial enough to suggest that a quarter of the district is actively open to alternative representation, and that margin can shift quickly if economic conditions deteriorate or the incumbent is perceived as unresponsive." },
          { name: "Assuming Cultural Homogeneity or Economic Stability", tag: "avoid", why: "The 88.23% white demographic does not indicate cultural uniformity or shared economic interests—a non-college-educated 49-year-old homeowner, a young service-sector worker, and a retiree on fixed income are all white but face fundamentally different economic circumstances and priorities. Any candidate or incumbent who campaigns this district as culturally or economically monolithic will miss the material economic divisions that are the actual terrain of competition. The moderate income level and lower educational attainment suggest economic fragility, not security—and economic fragility generates vulnerability to a challenger with a coherent economic message." }
