          { name: "Renter Majority & Housing Affordability", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 50.8% renters, VA-HD-78 is effectively a renter-majority district — an unusual demographic for a strongly Republican area, and a critical strategic vulnerability for any incumbent who treats housing policy as secondary. A median household income of $83,986 with a renter majority suggests professional-class renters who are financially stable but not property-wealthy, and who evaluate legislators on whether housing costs are stabilizing or accelerating relative to income. In a district this affluent and this educated, a renter-majority base signals a constituency that may be geographically mobile and responsive to cost-of-living concerns that incumbent comfort with existing policy may obscure." },
          { name: "The 19% Opposition & Professional Class Persuadability", tag: "careful", why: "An 81% Democratic result in a district with 67.5% college-educated professionals is a strong partisan margin, but the 19% opposition — concentrated among the college-educated professionals who would normally be Carr's base — merits analytical attention. At 35.2 median age, this is a younger professional constituency that may be evaluating on specific issues (education funding, healthcare policy, tax fairness) rather than inherited partisan alignment. The 19% opposition is likely concentrated among professionals in government, education, and healthcare sectors for whom Democratic positioning on these specific issues aligns with their professional interests — and any challenger capable of eroding 5-7 points from Carr's current margin would be viable in a rightly-timed competitive cycle." },
          { name: "Taking College-Educated Professional Consensus for Granted", tag: "avoid", why: "A 67.5% college-educated constituency in a professional-class district is not a permanent Democratic asset — it is a demographic cohort whose voting behavior is contingent on whether the representative is perceived as fluent on the specific policy concerns (education quality, healthcare access, tax structure, economic competitiveness) that drive professional-class electoral behavior. An incumbent who campaigns primarily on party identification rather than on specific professional-class economic concerns risks being outflanked by a challenger who engages these voters' actual concerns with greater specificity." },
          { name: "Treating the 50.8% Renter Base as a Secondary Constituency", tag: "avoid", why: "Housing affordability and renter protection are not peripheral issues in a district where half the households are renters earning $83,986 and face rising housing costs in a competitive market. An incumbent who frames housing policy as a secondary redistributive concern rather than as a core economic-competitiveness issue for the professional-class renters who are a key part of the 81% coalition risks ceding the issue to any challenger who speaks to housing affordability as a workforce-retention and quality-of-life priority. This is not a traditional renter-class issue — it is a professional-class economic-mobility issue." }
