          { name: "Working-Class Economic Security for a 49.7% Renter District", tag: "lean-into", why: "Nearly half of HD-93 rents, and the $64,971 median income means that housing costs, wage adequacy, childcare, and transportation are not policy abstractions but monthly budget constraints. The 95% Democratic result reflects constituent belief that Democratic governance better serves these material concerns — but that belief is contingent on seeing results in housing stability, wage growth, and cost-of-living relief. A representative who prioritizes tenant protections, minimum wage standards, childcare affordability, and transportation infrastructure is addressing the actual governing demand of the district's economic majority." },
          { name: "Black Majority Constituency Engagement & Accountability", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 48.56%, the Black majority is the electoral and demographic anchor of HD-93, and the 95% Democratic support reflects aligned policy priorities on criminal justice, economic equity, and community investment. The governing obligation is not to assume this support is permanent but to build specific, accountable relationships around concrete policy outcomes — police accountability, educational equity, workforce access, and wealth-building opportunities in homeownership and business formation. A representative who treats the Black constituency as a voting bloc to be managed rather than a community with distinct governance demands will find that 95% margin soften when that community perceives neglect or tokenism." },
          { name: "Educational Access & Workforce Development for 27.8% College-Educated Minority", tag: "careful", why: "The 27.8% college attainment rate is below state and national averages, signaling real constraints on educational access and professional mobility for the district's families. Community college funding, K-12 educational equity, and pre-professional workforce development are state-level policy levers that directly affect whether working-class families in HD-93 can move into higher-wage employment. This constituency is neither wealthy nor powerless — they are economically aspiring — and they will evaluate a representative partly on whether that person advances real educational opportunity or defaults to generic support for 'education' without material policy change." },
          { name: "Treating 95% as Permission to Disengage from Constituent Service", tag: "avoid", why: "A 95% margin is a supermajority, not a license to assume the constituency will remain engaged regardless of governance performance. The working-class, renter-majority, under-college-educated base voted 95% Democratic because they believed that choice serves their interests — not because they have no alternatives or cannot withdraw that support if they perceive neglect or broken promises. A representative who runs a safe seat as though the margin is permanent and begins the next cycle without intensive constituent service and accountability will find that margin eroded by either a challenger who offers a more specific economic agenda or by depressed turnout among a constituency that concludes the incumbent is not delivering." },
          { name: "Absorbing All Minority Communities into a Generic 'Democratic Coalition' Frame", tag: "avoid", why: "HD-93 has distinct Black (48.56%), Hispanic (9.71%), White (32.6%), and Asian (4.44%) communities with specific economic and social policy concerns that cannot be adequately addressed through generic minority-community outreach or assumed shared interests. The 95% result emerges from specific alignment between community priorities and Democratic policy on those distinct dimensions, not from abstract tribal affiliation. A representative who cannot articulate specific policy understanding of the economic concerns of the Black majority, the immigrant and service-sector workforce realities of the Hispanic community, and the homeownership and professional-class concerns of the White minority will miss governing opportunities and create openings for challengers who do." }
