          { name: "Treating 69% as a Permanent Safe Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "A 69-31 margin is substantial but not invulnerable, particularly in a district where the opposition includes three swing-capable constituencies: renters (31.6%), college-educated adults (26.9%), and Hispanic voters (10.25%). If renters consolidate around a candidate focused on housing affordability, if college-educated voters mobilize around education and healthcare, or if Hispanic voters turn out at higher rates around economic integration concerns, that 69% majority erodes quickly. Assuming demographic advantage is sufficient without active constituent engagement is the most reliable path to a vulnerable seat." },
          { name: "Dismissing the Demographic Gap as Immaterial", tag: "avoid", why: "The 3.19% gap between the four reported Census categories and 100% likely represents American Indian / Alaska Native residents whose political participation, civic infrastructure, and specific governance concerns (sovereignty, healthcare, economic development) are not captured in the partisan numbers. While small in percentage terms, this gap represents a distinct constituency whose engagement — or whose activation by a challenger offering specific commitments — could shift outcome in a race decided by margins. Ignoring this constituency amounts to governing without understanding the full composition of the district." }
