          "The district's economic profile demands specific attention. At $49,575 median household income — well below Virginia's state median — this is a financially-constrained constituency for whom healthcare costs, property tax burden, and employment stability are direct material concerns. The 45.6 median age and 25.4% renter rate, combined with the low college attainment, suggest a population anchored in blue-collar and service-sector work where wage stagnation, workforce retraining, and rural service availability are primary anxieties. The 93.53% white demographic dominance indicates that racial and identity-based political messaging may resonate, but it does not address the economic security questions that drive actual household decisions about representation. Any challenger who can credibly position themselves as defending working-class economic interests against cost-of-living pressure, healthcare access deterioration, or employment instability will have identified the actual vulnerability beneath the 97% surface — particularly if the current representative's record can be framed as defending elite interests over working-class household survival."
