          { name: "Suburban Housing Market & Renter Protections", tag: "lean-into", why: "With a 35.5% renter rate in an affluent district where median income is $86,936, VA-SD-11 has a sizable renter population within a homeowner-majority constituency — a profile that creates pressure on both housing affordability and tenant protections. Renters in this district are not low-income families but young professionals, graduate students, and service workers whose housing costs consume larger shares of income than in owner-occupied homes. Addressing housing affordability, tenant rights, and zoning flexibility without destabilizing homeowner equity is the practical governing challenge that this specific demographic mix creates." },
          { name: "Demographic Diversity Management in a Highly Educated District", tag: "careful", why: "The four reported demographic categories account for approximately 95.6% of the population, leaving a ~4.4% gap that likely reflects Asian Americans, multiracial residents, and other groups not fully captured in the four major categories. With 72.48% white, 12.13% Black, 6.32% Hispanic, and 4.68% Asian residents, the district is increasingly diverse but remains white-plurality, and the Asian undercount suggests model minority dynamics that can mask actual needs around language access, healthcare equity, and educational inclusion. A 66% Democratic margin in a district this educated and diverse is durable only if all communities perceive genuine engagement rather than token representation in education, policing, and economic opportunity." },
          { name: "Treating Renters as Secondary to Homeowners", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district where renters represent more than one-third of households, any candidate or incumbent who frames housing policy exclusively through homeowner equity preservation will alienate a sizable and politically active constituency whose material interests diverge from those of homeowners. At 35.5% renter rate, this is not a marginal constituency but a substantial bloc whose housing security and tenant rights are legitimate governance concerns equal to homeowner interests." },
          { name: "Defaulting to Partisan Talking Points Without Material Policy Specificity", tag: "avoid", why: "A 51.6% college-educated constituency with $86,936 median income is responsive to detailed policy discussion and skeptical of abstract partisan rhetoric divorced from evidence and implementation. The 66% Democratic margin is not a guarantee of continued support if the incumbent or challenger relies on ideological positioning rather than concrete answers to the district's specific challenges around school funding, housing affordability, and workforce pipeline development. This is a constituency that demands to understand *how* policy will work, not just *which party* supposedly supports it." }
