          { name: "Multiracial Coalition Building & Institutional Power-Sharing", tag: "lean-into", why: "The near-parity between white (45.3%) and Black (43%) constituencies in a college-educated, urban district creates both an opportunity and a vulnerability. Authentic power-sharing—reflected in staffing, budget priorities, whose voices shape agenda-setting, and whose community institutions receive institutional investment—is a governance obligation and an electoral asset. A representative who is perceived as serving one community's interests at the other's expense, or who treats the Black community as a voting bloc rather than a partner in power, risks eroding the coalition's durability even in a safe district." },
          { name: "Working-Class Economic Concerns Beyond College-Educated Professionals", tag: "careful", why: "While 47.4% college-educated is above average, 52.6% lack a four-year degree—a working- and service-class majority whose economic priorities (wage floors, workplace conditions, affordable childcare) may diverge from college-educated professionals' priorities (education funding, climate policy). In a supermajority district, it is possible for the representative's agenda to drift toward more educated constituencies' concerns while service-sector workers and working-class renters experience their priorities as neglected. This is not immediately electorally threatening at 95%, but it is a governance vulnerability and a foundation for future opposition." },
          { name: "Treating 95% as Permission to Govern Without Constituent Accountability", tag: "avoid", why: "A 95% result is not evidence that constituent needs are being met—it is evidence of current partisan alignment and satisfaction. In a district with 53.2% renters, 34.9 median age, and material economic diversity, supermajority margins can deteriorate rapidly if the representative is perceived as taking the constituency for granted, ignoring specific material concerns, or using power in ways that serve some communities at others' expense. The 5% opposition is not a measurement of failure; it is a floor, and it can rise if engagement declines or economic conditions worsen." },
          { name: "Assuming Education & Progressivism Are Sufficient Electoral Glue", tag: "avoid", why: "College education and progressive identification correlate with Democratic voting but do not guarantee durability, particularly in a young (34.9 median age) urban constituency where economic conditions and material outcomes shape electoral behavior more than inherited partisan loyalty. A representative whose agenda is organized around college-educated progressive constituencies' cultural and environmental priorities while ignoring service-sector workers' wage and housing concerns is vulnerable to erosion if younger, working-class constituents conclude power is not being used for their benefit. This is particularly acute in a renter-supermajority district where wealth-building and housing stability cannot be assumed." }
