          { name: "Education Quality and K-12 Funding", tag: "lean-into", why: "A district with 43.4% college attainment and $117,257 median income is home to professionals for whom school quality is inseparable from property values, family quality of life, and community choice — and who actively evaluate candidates on specific positions regarding K-12 funding adequacy, special education services, and the balance between public and choice-based school options. In Northern Virginia's competitive landscape, education policy is the single most durable predictor of school-district-based voting; a candidate who leads with clarity on K-12 priorities and funding strategy commands the attention of the district's affluent family base." },
          { name: "Property Tax Fairness and Fiscal Responsibility", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 80.4% homeownership and $117k median income, voters in VA-SD-19 are paying substantial property taxes and want assurance that government is managing growth, controlling spending, and delivering value for what they pay — not abstract fiscal conservatism, but specific, credible positions on tax structure, spending priorities, and whether local government is responsive to resident concerns. Voters in this income and ownership bracket will reward candidates who demonstrate genuine fiscal stewardship and penalize those who appear indifferent to taxpayer burden or growth management." },
          { name: "Healthcare Access and Women's Economic Concerns", tag: "careful", why: "The 42% Democratic share in a district with majority college-educated voters and high female professional participation suggests meaningful numbers of educated women evaluating candidates on healthcare access, reproductive policy, and whether government is supporting or constraining women's economic opportunity and autonomy. This constituency is persuadable but attentive; candidates who dismiss these concerns as ideological rather than engaging them as substantive policy issues will lose credibility with college-educated female professionals whose economic stakes in the district are real." },
          { name: "Assuming 58% is a Stable Floor", tag: "avoid", why: "Northern Virginia's affluent suburban voters, particularly those with college education and professional status, have demonstrated increasing independence from inherited partisan loyalty and increasing willingness to evaluate candidates on governance record and specific policy positioning rather than party label. A 58% result in a district this educated and professionally mobile is not a durable coalition unless actively maintained through credible engagement on voters' material concerns; treating it as safe encourages the inattention that vulnerable incumbents make when they mistake current margins for future stability." },
          { name: "Positioning School and Tax Policy as Secondary Issues", tag: "avoid", why: "A candidate who treats education funding and property tax policy as peripheral to their message in a district where 80% own homes, 43% are college-educated, and median income is $117k is misreading the constituency's priorities and ceding the most durable persuasion leverage to a challenger willing to lead on these issues. For affluent suburban professionals, these are not secondary concerns tacked onto a broader ideological message — they are the primary material stakes that determine quality of life and community choice, and they must be front-and-center in any credible campaign." }
