          { name: "Black Community Turnout & Electoral Preference", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 27.43% of the district, the Black population is large enough that shifts in turnout or candidate preference of 2-3 percentage points directly change the overall result. A 55% Democratic win in a district this diverse suggests either soft Black voter engagement or meaningful Republican crossover in Black precincts — both indicate vulnerability. Building explicit constituent relationships around education quality, healthcare access, criminal justice, and economic opportunity in Black neighborhoods, rather than assuming these voters as a base, is the most direct path to expanding the Democratic margin in SD-22." },
          { name: "Renter Economic Anxiety & Housing Affordability", tag: "careful", why: "The 36.4% renter rate is substantially higher than suburban Virginia norms and reflects a constituency for whom housing cost burden is a primary household budget concern — particularly in a district with rising professional-class rents driven by demand from the $81,964+ income cohort. Renters are significantly more volatile voters than homeowners and more responsive to messaging on economic affordability and cost-of-living pressure. The incumbent must offer specific, credible policy remedies on housing supply, tenant protections, and affordability rather than abstract support for housing advocates — and must demonstrate that Democratic governance is actually producing housing outcomes, not just promises." },
          { name: "Republican Messaging on Taxes & Regulation in Affluent Precincts", tag: "avoid", why: "A 45% Republican result in a district with above-median income and college attainment reflects effective Republican messaging on tax burden and regulatory burden — messaging that lands with particular force among healthcare employers, small professional services firms, and high-earner households. Dismissing or ignoring this messaging as ideologically incoherent risks ceding affluent neighborhoods to Republican candidates who are effectively competing for these voters. The incumbent must either refute the messaging with specificity or offer an alternative economic governing vision that affluent professionals find credible." },
          { name: "Taking 55% as a Safe Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "A 55-45 margin in a district this educated, diverse, and renter-heavy is not a durable floor — it is a plurality of contested voters in a state-level Democratic year. Asian voters at 9.52%, young professionals at 35.8-year median age, and the 36.4% renter population are all cohorts capable of significant mobilization or demobilization based on incumbent performance on their specific material concerns. Any incumbent who campaigns this seat as though 55% represents a comfortable hold, rather than as a margin that requires active consolidation of each key constituency, will find the result vulnerable to challenge in a cycle where Democratic turnout is softer or Republican messaging on economics is more resonant." }
