          { name: "Treating 62% as a Safe Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "A 62-38 result in a district this educationally advanced and geographically proximate to Democratic-voting Northern Virginia suburbs is not a durable safe seat — it is a working margin in a rapidly evolving region where college-educated suburban voters are genuinely persuadable on economic governance. Any incumbent or Republican challenger who campaigns this district as though 62% represents a comfortable hold will be caught off guard if a credible Democratic challenger emerges with messaging on fiscal responsibility, education, or infrastructure that appeals to the college-educated constituency. The structural fragility of college-educated suburban Republican voting should override the comfort of the current 24-point margin." },
          { name: "Dismissing Democratic Opposition as Locked in an Ideological Minority", tag: "avoid", why: "The 38% Democratic share in a district where 41.4% are college-educated, median income is $105,138, and the district sits in the Washington metro sphere is not a floor of partisan Democrats — it is a coalition of affluent professionals who have made an economic and cultural realignment toward Democratic voting on education, infrastructure, and fiscal sustainability issues. Framing this opposition as ideologically marginal or unpersuadable will cause a candidate to miss the actual economic concerns driving Democratic voting behavior, and will fail to engage the persuadable college-educated swing voters who determine close elections in this district." }
