          "The district's economic profile — median age 45.3, 41.4% college-educated, median income $105,138, renter rate only 17.4% — describes affluent, settled professionals with strong financial stakes in property values, market stability, and tax policy. This is a constituency that votes on economic self-interest and fiscal governance, not cultural grievance, and it will defect from a candidate perceived as economically reckless or fiscally incompetent. Mcdougle's 62% performance was built on solid Republican positioning among this group, but the 38% Democratic share represents a real competitive floor in a district where educational attainment (41.4%) and proximity to the Democratic-leaning Washington metro have created a meaningful opposition coalition. Any incumbent or challenger who assumes the 62% represents a durable Republican base will be caught off guard if a credible Democratic challenger emerges with a message anchored on fiscal responsibility, transportation infrastructure, or education funding — issues where college-educated suburban voters are genuinely persuadable. The median age of 45.3 and homeownership concentration also signal a constituency focused on retirement security, healthcare costs, and whether schools and services support their property values, not abstract ideological positioning."
