          { name: "Tax Policy & Service Delivery Efficiency", tag: "lean-into", why: "At $131,667 household income with 79.9% homeownership, SD-29 is a constituency paying substantial state and local taxes and evaluating whether that tax burden is generating commensurate service delivery in schools, infrastructure, and public safety. The 44% Republican opposition in an affluent, educated district is not a base defection but swing voters concluding that tax burden is excessive relative to service quality or that government is inefficient. A governing majority in this district requires demonstrating that public investment is producing measurable returns — not abstract arguments about tax fairness, but specific evidence that schools are competitive, infrastructure is maintained, and public safety is visible and effective." },
          { name: "Professional & Entrepreneurial Constituency Heterogeneity", tag: "careful", why: "With Hispanic (24.85%), Asian (9.09%), and Black (21.72%) populations totaling 55.66%, SD-29 includes distinct professional and entrepreneurial communities — immigrant small business owners, second-generation Black professionals, and Asian immigrant and native-born professionals — whose economic interests and governance priorities differ significantly. Hispanic and Asian constituents in this income band are often concerned with regulatory burden on small business, immigration policy predictability, and tax policy; Black constituents often prioritize economic mobility, neighborhood investment, and representation in leadership roles. Treating these constituencies as a unified diversity bloc rather than engaging their distinct economic interests risks losing the swing voters within each group who are evaluating primarily on whether their specific needs are being addressed." },
          { name: "Assuming Demographic Diversity Produces Automatic Democratic Loyalty", tag: "avoid", why: "The gap between SD-29's demographic composition (55.66% non-white, 42.1% college-educated, $131,667 income) and the actual 56% Democratic margin reveals that demographic diversity does not automatically produce strong Democratic performance in affluent, educated districts. These voters are swing voters evaluating on governance performance, tax efficiency, and service delivery — not on racial representation or abstract diversity metrics. Any candidate who campaigns as though demographic diversity is sufficient to secure this constituency, or who frames opposition as lacking diversity rather than diagnosing specific governance failures, is misreading the electorate and vulnerable to a challenger who offers concrete solutions to the performance gaps that generated the 44% opposition." },
          { name: "Treating 56% as a Durable Ceiling", tag: "avoid", why: "A 56% result in a district this educated, prosperous, and diverse is not a floor or a safe majority — it is the outcome of specific governance choices or policy positions that have convinced swing voters to support the incumbent. In a district where 44% of voters chose an alternative, any incumbent who campaigns as though 56% represents a stable base and who neglects the underlying reasons why 44% voted differently is arithmetically misunderstanding the race. The 20.1% renter rate and 35.3-year median age also signal mobile, less rooted constituencies that can shift more readily than older homeowner-heavy districts — treating this as a safe seat is a recipe for a competitive challenge in the next cycle." }
