          { name: "Young, Affluent, Educated Metro Constituency", tag: "lean-into", why: "VA-SD-30's median age of 36.1, median household income of $126,752, and 42.7% college attainment reflect a relatively young, highly educated, and economically secure Northern Virginia metro constituency. This demographic profile typically aligns with engagement on climate policy, education investment, housing affordability despite high incomes, and local government efficiency — issues that resonate with knowledge workers and young professionals in the Arlington area. A legislative agenda that takes these community priorities seriously, rather than treating the district as a conservative-leaning suburban base, better reflects the actual composition and values of voters who delivered a 52% Democratic result in 2025." },
          { name: "Renter & Housing Affordability Pressure", tag: "careful", why: "At 26.9%, VA-SD-30's renter rate is substantially above Virginia state average and signals a meaningful constituency of non-homeowners in what is otherwise an affluent district. In Northern Virginia's high-cost market, renters at median household incomes of $126,752 are likely young professionals, service-sector workers, and immigrant families priced out of ownership despite significant earnings. This constituency can swing based on housing affordability, tenant protections, and whether they perceive government as working in their economic interest — an issue that separates affluent renters from comfortable homeowners and that has proven electorally significant in competitive Northern Virginia seats." },
          { name: "Treating the 48% Opposition as a Stable Conservative Base", tag: "avoid", why: "A 52-48 Democratic margin is not a mandate — it is a tie in a district that the incumbent won by 4 percentage points. Any Democrat who campaigns as though the 52% represents a consolidated progressive coalition and the 48% represents an immovable conservative base is misreading the district's actual dynamics. The presence of 30.78% Hispanic residents, a 26.9% renter rate, and a young, educated metro constituency all signal fluidity; the thin margin suggests that opposition voters are persuadable or discouraged, not ideologically fixed." },
          { name: "Framing as a Safe Democratic Seat", tag: "avoid", why: "VA-SD-30 delivered a 52% Democratic result in 2025 — a narrow win in a state Senate district that, in Virginia's increasingly competitive statewide environment, is well within the margin of error for any number of 2027 or 2029 swings. Northern Virginia has demonstrated capacity for ticket-splitting and swing behavior when local governance concerns outweigh partisan alignment; any incumbent or challenger who assumes the 52% represents a durable Democratic floor rather than a narrow, persuadable constituency will be unprepared for a credible challenge from the opposition." }
