          { name: "Public Education Excellence & School Funding", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 70.7% college attainment and median age 37.9, VA-SD-37 is a constituency organized around educational achievement and children's academic outcomes. Public school performance, advanced placement access, STEM programming, and equitable resource distribution across schools in the district are the direct quality-of-life metrics that determine whether highly educated, affluent parents perceive their representative as delivering on core governance priorities. Any candidate who cannot speak with specificity about school funding mechanisms, teacher compensation, and enrollment management loses credibility with the district's dominant constituency." },
          { name: "Professional-Class Economic Opportunity & Market Access", tag: "lean-into", why: "At $151,442 median household income, VA-SD-37 is a district of economically successful professionals whose continued prosperity depends on robust job markets, professional infrastructure investment, and economic growth. Technology sector viability, professional services capacity, transportation infrastructure enabling commute efficiency, and tax policy that preserves disposable income for this high-earning population are the governance concerns that determine incumbent performance evaluation. A representative who demonstrates understanding of professional-class economic concerns and delivers policy that sustains economic opportunity is consolidating the 69% base; one who prioritizes redistributive messaging over growth and opportunity risks making that coalition swing-eligible." },
          { name: "Asian American Constituency Engagement & Education-Economic Alignment", tag: "careful", why: "The 21.45% Asian American population is substantially above national average and represents a significant swing constituency within the district's professional class. Asian American voters in high-income suburban districts are economically pragmatic, responsive to education and public safety performance metrics, and capable of voting across partisan lines if they perceive incumbent mismanagement or superior challenger competence. This constituency is neither reliably Democratic nor inherently Republican; its alignment with current Democratic governance reflects satisfaction with incumbent performance on schools, public safety, and economic management — not inherited partisan loyalty. Losing focus on education quality, public safety, or economic competence creates swing opportunity here." },
          { name: "Treating 69% as Insurmountable Democratic Dominance", tag: "avoid", why: "A 69% result in a highly educated, economically successful district reflects current incumbent performance on education, competence, and economic management — not structural Democratic advantage. The district's demographic composition and professional-class character mean that voters evaluate on policy specificity and results, not cultural messaging or partisan identity. An incumbent who assumes the 69% is durable and neglects constituent service, education outcomes, or economic stewardship risks finding that margin erode rapidly; a challenger who demonstrates superior competence on these metrics can credibly contest this seat." },
          { name: "Redistributive Messaging Over Growth & Opportunity", tag: "avoid", why: "A constituency with $151,442 median income and 70.7% college attainment evaluates on whether representatives understand professional-class economic interests and deliver policy that sustains growth, opportunity, and tax efficiency — not on whether the incumbent voices progressive cultural positioning. Framing governance around redistribution, wealth taxation, or economic constraints rather than around education quality, professional opportunity, and competent economic management risks alienating the educated, affluent professionals who form the electoral base. A candidate who positions against the district's material interests (strong schools, economic growth, professional opportunity) as defined by the constituency, rather than against incumbent performance on these metrics, loses credibility with voters who evaluate on results." }
